1
00:00:24,179 --> 00:00:30,005
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOC's course on Foundations of Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:33,049 --> 00:00:50,928
We are going to start discussion on a new topic that is our other senses other than visual and auditory sense and their role in full immersive experience.

3
00:00:50,948 --> 00:00:52,870
So, previously what we have covered.

4
00:00:53,170 --> 00:01:00,217
We have talked about the relation between human physiology and the usage of AR-VR systems.

5
00:01:00,818 --> 00:01:03,040
That was at the beginning of this module.

6
00:01:03,941 --> 00:01:18,216
Then we talked about in detail, in some detail, not too much detail, but in some detail, the human visual perceptual system and how the knowledge of human

7
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:24,922
perceptual system, visual perceptual system helps in building better XR systems.

8
00:01:26,164 --> 00:01:37,979
Subsequently, we talked about the human auditory system and how it helps create immersive experience in XR in addition to the visual perceptual system.

9
00:01:39,682 --> 00:01:45,149
Now, we know that we have five senses plus a sixth sense.

10
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:46,991
What are those five senses?

11
00:01:47,011 --> 00:01:48,253
Visual, auditory,

12
00:01:48,723 --> 00:01:58,735
touch sense, smell, taste and the sixth sense as we have discussed earlier is the proprioceptive sense or proprioception.

13
00:01:59,836 --> 00:02:10,149
So, in this lecture, we are going to cover the remaining senses that are touch, taste, smell and proprioception and their relation to XR.

14
00:02:10,189 --> 00:02:11,410
So, how do we perceive them?

15
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:17,177
What is the physiology that enables us to perceive?

16
00:02:17,478 --> 00:02:26,831
these different things like touch or taste or smell and how that knowledge helps in building better XR systems.

17
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:33,541
So, we are going to cover that in this lecture.

18
00:02:33,561 --> 00:02:37,706
Let us begin with the sense of touch and proprioception.

19
00:02:37,786 --> 00:02:38,768
These two are connected.

20
00:02:38,828 --> 00:02:40,330
So, we will first talk about them.

21
00:02:43,212 --> 00:02:52,184
Like the human visual perceptual system or human auditory perceptual system, we have the human somatosensory system.

22
00:02:53,185 --> 00:02:57,250
Now this is another sensory organ, the somatosensory system.

23
00:02:58,352 --> 00:03:07,364
It handles touch, proprioception, and kinesthesis.

24
00:03:07,384 --> 00:03:11,329
Now proprioception, we have already talked about it before.

25
00:03:11,832 --> 00:03:27,587
This is nothing but awareness of the pose of each movable human body part such as legs, arms, fingers, tongue, mouth and lips, whichever can be moved relative to other body parts.

26
00:03:28,088 --> 00:03:37,317
So, when we are moving any of these body parts, we do it consciously with full knowledge of

27
00:03:37,651 --> 00:03:45,699
what we are doing, that awareness is called proprioception or our sixth sense.

28
00:03:45,719 --> 00:03:55,308
Kinesthesis, on the other hand, provides information on velocities, acceleration, and forces while moving these body parts.

29
00:03:55,869 --> 00:03:58,591
So, proprioception is awareness of movement.

30
00:03:58,731 --> 00:04:04,637
Kinesthesis is awareness of various parameters associated with the movement.

31
00:04:08,397 --> 00:04:25,359
Now, in the human somatosensory system, we have at least nine major kinds of receptors, out of which six are fully devoted to touch senses,

32
00:04:25,359 --> 00:04:32,650
the sense of touch, and the remaining three are devoted to proprioception and kinesthesis.

33
00:04:33,592 --> 00:04:42,244
So essentially, we have this human somatosensory system responsible for our sense of touch as well as proprioception and kinesthesis.

34
00:04:47,232 --> 00:04:49,375
Let us talk about the touch receptors.

35
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:52,319
So these receptors are embedded in the skin.

36
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:59,088
Now skin has different layers such as epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis.

37
00:04:59,108 --> 00:05:00,190
Now these

38
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:14,764
These receptors, touch receptors, are embedded in the dermis and epidermis layers, that is, the first and second layers which are just below the skin.

39
00:05:19,112 --> 00:05:24,321
As you can see, there are many such receptors, at least six.

40
00:05:25,145 --> 00:05:41,969
such as Merkel's discs, hair follicle receptors, free nerve endings, Ruffini's endings, Meissner's corpuscles, Pacinian corpuscles, and so on.

41
00:05:45,354 --> 00:05:53,886
So, all of these you can see are part of either the dermis or epidermis layer of the skin.

42
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:05,689
For proprioception, we use a different set of receptors, although they are part of the somatosensory system.

43
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:14,419
Now, one of those are the muscle spindles, which are embedded inside each muscle.

44
00:06:15,881 --> 00:06:20,606
These spindles report to the brain on changes in length.

45
00:06:20,727 --> 00:06:27,214
So, whenever there is some movement, some change in length happens in those muscles, and those changes are reported to the brain.

46
00:06:27,667 --> 00:06:33,473
by these muscle spindles which are kinds of receptors for the proprioception sense.

47
00:06:36,615 --> 00:06:39,698
Then we have Golgi tendon organs.

48
00:06:43,002 --> 00:06:57,235
These are embedded in tendons, which are tough bands of fibrous tissues connecting muscles to bones, and these tendon organs are supposed to report changes in muscle tension.

49
00:06:58,143 --> 00:07:00,767
which are associated with movement of muscles.

50
00:07:04,012 --> 00:07:08,158
Also, there are joint receptors which lie at the joints between bones.

51
00:07:08,699 --> 00:07:17,661
So, we have receptors at muscle, muscle receptors at connection between muscle and bone, and also at bone joints we have receptors,

52
00:07:17,661 --> 00:07:27,087
joint receptors which help coordinate muscle movement and they provide information on relative bone positions.

53
00:07:27,893 --> 00:07:40,982
So, all these receptors, so-called receptors, help us get a sense of movements in our body parts with respect to other body parts as well as

54
00:07:40,982 --> 00:07:45,773
the properties of that movement such as acceleration, velocity, and so on.

55
00:07:49,977 --> 00:07:55,403
So, that is in brief what the somatosensory system is that is responsible for

56
00:07:56,513 --> 00:08:04,322
the sense of touch as well as proprioception and how the physiology is there in our body.

57
00:08:04,762 --> 00:08:15,074
Now, let us try to understand how it works in terms of a very simple example: suppose our hand touches an object.

58
00:08:15,414 --> 00:08:24,124
So, this example will briefly talk about the touch sense although it is equally applicable for the proprioceptive sense.

59
00:08:26,348 --> 00:08:28,551
Suppose our hand touches an object.

60
00:08:29,172 --> 00:08:30,074
So, then what happens?

61
00:08:30,675 --> 00:08:37,486
So, the somatosensory receptors get activated once the hand touches the object.

62
00:08:37,506 --> 00:08:48,002
And those receptors, activation of those receptors, starts a chain of events by sending a signal to the nearest neuron.

63
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:58,964
Then the neuron transmits a message to the next neuron.

64
00:08:59,084 --> 00:09:08,574
As we know, there is this neural network meant for transferring neural information from one neuron to another.

65
00:09:09,895 --> 00:09:12,038
The same thing happens in our somatosensory system.

66
00:09:12,558 --> 00:09:24,250
So the receptors receive this input and then transform it to a neural impulse, which gets transmitted through the neural network.

67
00:09:24,652 --> 00:09:30,679
from one neuron to the next neuron till the impulse reaches the brain.

68
00:09:32,501 --> 00:09:39,630
Now, the brain processes the message, whatever is coming through the neural network, which gives us the touch perception.

69
00:09:40,551 --> 00:09:49,461
Also, the brain sends some messages back to the hand in this case via the same pathway.

70
00:09:50,504 --> 00:09:56,213
to take corrective motor action if needed, such as removing hand from hot object.

71
00:09:56,233 --> 00:10:01,861
So, it is not only about receiving but also acting on the input.

72
00:10:01,881 --> 00:10:12,698
So, once the information is processed, then the brain also sends some commands to perform the motor action necessary for the situation.

73
00:10:19,613 --> 00:10:23,761
So, that is how the somatosensory perception works.

74
00:10:24,301 --> 00:10:38,207
Now, a major part of the somatosensory perception is called haptic perception, which is the perception of the overall geometric shape of objects.

75
00:10:38,607 --> 00:10:43,917
Now, this overall shape of objects can be inferred through haptic exploration.

76
00:10:47,288 --> 00:10:49,271
which involves object handling.

77
00:10:49,592 --> 00:11:00,110
So, one is touching, the other one is handling or manipulation of objects to infer the overall shape of the object, geometric shape through haptic perception.

78
00:11:00,471 --> 00:11:03,837
That is also very much part of our perception of touch.

79
00:11:06,882 --> 00:11:11,490
Now, haptic exploration or the manipulation of geometric objects to infer

80
00:11:12,483 --> 00:11:22,716
through our perception process to infer the overall shape of the object. Such explorations can be of six types.

81
00:11:23,758 --> 00:11:41,781
As illustrated in this figure, we have lateral motion texture to perceive the texture of object surface through lateral motion, then pressure,

82
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:51,974
hardness by pinching on the surfaces. We can understand the hardness of the surface.

83
00:11:51,994 --> 00:11:55,719
So, lateral motion is meant for texture understanding or texture perception.

84
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:59,024
Pressure is meant for hardness perception.

85
00:11:59,625 --> 00:12:08,578
Then enclosure is meant for global shape or volume perception, that is putting our hands around the object in the way shown here.

86
00:12:10,685 --> 00:12:16,154
Static contact can lead to temperature perception just by touching the object surface.

87
00:12:17,716 --> 00:12:25,488
Unsupported holding can be used to perceive the weight of the object as shown here.

88
00:12:26,450 --> 00:12:35,123
And contour following by moving our fingers around the surfaces, we can perceive the shape.

89
00:12:36,622 --> 00:12:41,449
So, there are six things that we can perceive using haptic exploration.

90
00:12:41,529 --> 00:12:54,809
Those are texture, hardness, shape, temperature, weight, and also shape.

91
00:12:57,272 --> 00:12:59,415
In one case, it is global shape.

92
00:12:59,696 --> 00:13:01,178
In another case, it is local shape.

93
00:13:04,887 --> 00:13:10,774
And for each of these perceptions, we need to perform a different type of haptic exploration.

94
00:13:10,834 --> 00:13:24,683
For example, lateral motion is meant for texture perception, pressure as shown here is meant for hardness perception, enclosure as shown here is meant for global shape perception,

95
00:13:24,683 --> 00:13:33,122
static contact meant for temperature perception, unsupported holding meant for

96
00:13:33,507 --> 00:13:39,396
weight perception, and finally contour following is meant for local shape perception as shown here.

97
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:50,573
So, these are means to perceive different geometric properties of objects that are there in our surroundings.

98
00:13:52,376 --> 00:13:55,981
So, the perception can be thought of at different levels.

99
00:13:56,161 --> 00:13:59,346
One is there are receptors in the human physiology,

100
00:14:00,845 --> 00:14:02,147
that is physiology of skin.

101
00:14:02,767 --> 00:14:13,681
Now these receptors help us get input from the real world and then process the information to perceive something through touch.

102
00:14:13,701 --> 00:14:24,355
Now to have a better perception, higher level perception along with simply touch, we need to perform some manipulations of objects.

103
00:14:25,870 --> 00:14:28,374
Those are called haptic explorations.

104
00:14:28,394 --> 00:14:39,670
And as we have just discussed, there can be six such types of explorations to obtain or perceive geometric features, different geometric features.

105
00:14:45,199 --> 00:14:52,890
Also, it may be noted that each of these explorations through which we perceive different properties of objects,

106
00:14:53,073 --> 00:14:56,237
involves different kinds of receptors.

107
00:15:01,504 --> 00:15:06,712
And also they involve combination of spatial and temporal information.

108
00:15:06,732 --> 00:15:19,369
So it is not only spatial information, but also temporal information is involved for perception of the object properties through haptic exploration.

109
00:15:19,389 --> 00:15:22,573
Now the overall goal of this haptic exploration is

110
00:15:23,077 --> 00:15:34,922
is by integrating the somatosensory signals from this in-hand manipulation, the manipulation of the object as shown in the figure,

111
00:15:34,922 --> 00:15:47,524
A geometric model of the object is learned in terms of its properties, such as shape, size, weight, hardness, and so on.

112
00:15:49,907 --> 00:15:50,788
That is the overall

113
00:15:51,646 --> 00:15:53,368
idea of this manipulation.

114
00:15:53,408 --> 00:16:11,393
So, when we try to perceive something, we create a model of the object, and to aid our model-building exercise, our mental model-building exercise, we perform haptic exploration.

115
00:16:11,413 --> 00:16:21,147
So, as can be figured out from the preceding discussion, clearly this haptic perception

116
00:16:22,021 --> 00:16:34,408
It is very important to create a mental model of the world surrounding us, and it also implies that such mental model creation, which is an essential part of

117
00:16:34,408 --> 00:16:43,692
an immersive experience, can be beneficial for better

118
00:16:44,178 --> 00:16:45,980
immersive experience.

119
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:52,926
So, this haptic exploration or haptic perception offers an extra dimension to the VR world.

120
00:16:53,326 --> 00:17:00,593
We can perceive the world better if we allow haptic exploration in the world.

121
00:17:02,234 --> 00:17:14,185
By letting users feel the virtual environment via the sense of touch or, in other words, via haptic exploration, we can let the users have a better feeling of the virtual world

122
00:17:15,211 --> 00:17:19,877
and also help one feel truly immersed in the artificial world.

123
00:17:20,919 --> 00:17:29,571
In our real world, when we say that we are immersed in the real world, that involves this haptic exploration as well.

124
00:17:29,611 --> 00:17:41,067
And if we allow the same thing in the virtual world, then certainly that will increase the immersive experience, that will better the immersive experience.

125
00:17:45,333 --> 00:17:57,246
For example, imagine yourself in a desert in a virtual world, in an artificial setting, seeing the sand and feeling it glide under your feet as you walk.

126
00:17:57,707 --> 00:18:03,053
Now, that is a haptic touch perception.

127
00:18:05,175 --> 00:18:11,863
If we want to give a user a true feeling of walking on the sand in a desert,

128
00:18:12,164 --> 00:18:18,773
then certainly only visual or auditory perception may not give a complete picture to the user.

129
00:18:18,973 --> 00:18:31,166
Instead, if we can add some haptic exploration support in the virtual system through the use of proper hardware and software,

130
00:18:31,166 --> 00:18:40,382
that experience will be far better as compared to an experience involving only visual or auditory senses.

131
00:18:43,450 --> 00:19:03,120
Now, when you say we want to provide this touch sensation, it can be provided through engineered devices, and there are many disparate systems to provide the experience.

132
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:09,610
Typically, such devices provide, in the most popular form, force feedback

133
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:14,323
in the form of vibrations from XR applications.

134
00:19:15,644 --> 00:19:25,781
So, typically the most popular way of providing touch sensation stimuli as output is through force feedback devices and force feedback,

135
00:19:25,781 --> 00:19:34,903
which are quite common in many of the XR systems, particularly VR systems.

136
00:19:34,923 --> 00:19:41,149
For example, force feedback mouse, smart gloves, or body suits which allow

137
00:19:42,277 --> 00:19:47,024
the user to experience the force feedback.

138
00:19:50,169 --> 00:20:01,832
An interesting example can be the KGS dot view model DV-2, which is a haptic pin array as shown here; as you can see, this part is containing

139
00:20:01,832 --> 00:20:08,456
an array of pins, a matrix of pins, which are controlled by the system by the computer.

140
00:20:10,018 --> 00:20:11,440
Now, pins are forced

141
00:20:11,892 --> 00:20:17,860
upward to simulate various textures as the fingertip scans across its surface.

142
00:20:19,562 --> 00:20:27,667
So, this pin array can be controlled by the computer, and depending on the objects we are dealing with in the virtual world,

143
00:20:27,667 --> 00:20:39,688
the corresponding signals will be generated to force the pins upward so that the user gets a feeling of touching an actual object, getting a feel on the fingers or hand.

144
00:20:41,305 --> 00:20:43,288
of the texture of the object surface.

145
00:20:47,875 --> 00:21:00,082
But this type of pin array device and controlling them precisely to give the realistic feeling of a textured surface, as we can understand, is not very easy,

146
00:21:00,082 --> 00:21:03,499
very difficult, generally difficult and challenging.

147
00:21:08,542 --> 00:21:11,466
There can be other ways to resolve this issue.

148
00:21:12,928 --> 00:21:17,835
Now, these haptic devices are actively controlled by a computer.

149
00:21:17,875 --> 00:21:30,912
However, to give the user the feeling of touching a real object surface while in the virtual world, an alternative can be to rely on real objects to provide feedback to the somatosensory system.

150
00:21:31,433 --> 00:21:38,042
So, we can allow the user to use real objects whose virtual avatars are there in the

151
00:21:38,832 --> 00:21:53,735
world, and that is generally considered to be less challenging than actually coming up with a computer-controlled haptic device.

152
00:21:54,877 --> 00:22:04,232
Now, this type of real-world object replacing the computer-controlled haptic devices for

153
00:22:04,718 --> 00:22:09,506
touch sensation, they are sometimes called tangible user interfaces.

154
00:22:11,829 --> 00:22:19,943
Now, when we are trying to implement that alternative, the idea is very simple.

155
00:22:20,203 --> 00:22:25,832
We need to make the experience by aligning the real and virtual worlds.

156
00:22:27,213 --> 00:22:31,870
For example, a see-through display such as Microsoft HoloLens which is

157
00:22:31,870 --> 00:22:40,888
typically good for mixed reality or augmented reality experience enables users to see and interact with the physical world around.

158
00:22:42,530 --> 00:22:43,471
There is no issue with that.

159
00:22:44,172 --> 00:22:51,139
Now, the display simply adds virtual objects to the real world or visually enhances real objects.

160
00:22:53,742 --> 00:22:54,503
So, when we are

161
00:22:56,407 --> 00:23:06,288
Seeing the real object, we are interacting with it; the computer also sees it through our wearable device sensors, and it detects which real object we are dealing with,

162
00:23:06,288 --> 00:23:18,429
Then accordingly it may change something in the view of the real object or it may add virtual objects to the real world.

163
00:23:23,014 --> 00:23:25,536
So, that is about the haptic

164
00:23:26,377 --> 00:23:31,263
or touch sense and how it can be useful in XR systems.

165
00:23:31,863 --> 00:23:37,330
Next, let us move our attention to the idea of taste and smell.

166
00:23:41,114 --> 00:23:47,522
Now, we are discussing them together because generally they are grouped together as chemical senses.

167
00:23:47,702 --> 00:23:57,385
Unlike the previous cases where we have used different types of receptors, here chemoreceptors are more important.

168
00:23:57,385 --> 00:24:09,279
So, they are generally called chemical senses because the receptors, the chemoreceptors work by chemical interaction with the molecules that arrive on them.

169
00:24:10,080 --> 00:24:26,556
So, taste, sense of taste or sense of smell is nothing but chemical reaction of the chemoreceptors with the stimuli that are arriving to the receptors.

170
00:24:29,506 --> 00:24:40,980
These chemoreceptors are capable of responding to particular substances at sufficiently high levels of concentration, appropriate amount of concentration rather to speak.

171
00:24:42,182 --> 00:24:48,570
So, chemoreceptors are designed to interact with specific chemicals, specific chemical stimuli.

172
00:24:49,050 --> 00:24:53,296
So, not all chemoreceptors are good for dealing with all sorts of chemical stimuli.

173
00:24:57,261 --> 00:24:59,243
Unlike the other three senses,

174
00:24:59,527 --> 00:25:13,244
say visual, auditory and touch senses, there is much less work, much less research done with respect to the sense of smell and taste and how to integrate them in XR systems.

175
00:25:15,166 --> 00:25:26,040
And even fewer electronic devices were made or are being made that display or that actually deal with stimuli.

176
00:25:26,729 --> 00:25:33,359
to nose and tongue which are the body organs responsible for smell and taste.

177
00:25:36,164 --> 00:25:42,574
Now let us first try to see what has been done so far in terms of smell interfaces.

178
00:25:43,856 --> 00:25:51,247
Now adding scent or smell to films is actually a very old idea.

179
00:25:51,748 --> 00:25:54,913
It can be traced back to the early 20th century.

180
00:25:58,943 --> 00:26:03,731
During our discussion on history, we talked about various systems.

181
00:26:03,751 --> 00:26:20,718
One such system was Smell-O-Vision, developed way back in 1960, which injected 30 different odors, smells, into movie theater seats at different points during the film.

182
00:26:20,798 --> 00:26:25,305
Viewers were seated at different points,

183
00:26:25,859 --> 00:26:40,363
In the film, depending on the scenery, different odors were sprayed to the seat so that viewers could smell and feel immersed in that particular scene.

184
00:26:40,383 --> 00:26:45,232
The other system we talked about, Sensorama, also included smells.

185
00:26:49,178 --> 00:26:52,103
So, what does a smell interface look like?

186
00:26:53,399 --> 00:27:00,727
Which actually is meant for providing stimuli to our nose.

187
00:27:00,747 --> 00:27:13,821
It usually involves air pumps that can spray chemical compounds into air, could be delivered close to the nose for a personal experience.

188
00:27:16,223 --> 00:27:17,765
One such schematic is shown here.

189
00:27:19,867 --> 00:27:21,769
So this odor is sprayed.

190
00:27:22,323 --> 00:27:34,600
Near the nose using the micropump setup the odor comes to the nose and there are other components which help create this odor.

191
00:27:37,945 --> 00:27:48,499
So, for personal experience it is sprayed near the nose of the user or for a social or larger experience it can be delivered on the scale of a room.

192
00:27:49,981 --> 00:27:53,584
Say for example, somebody is experiencing VR in a cave system.

193
00:27:53,664 --> 00:27:59,390
So, there that is a room and at room level it can be done.

194
00:28:03,874 --> 00:28:12,081
But clearly it is generally hard to control the intensity and uniformity of the odor across the room.

195
00:28:13,022 --> 00:28:18,391
So, it is relatively easier at an individual scale to generate,

196
00:28:18,391 --> 00:28:24,233
and create the effect as compared to a room level effect.

197
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:41,477
What is typically desirable in such cases is to vary the concentration of odors over a large area so that localization can be performed, which is again difficult to achieve with accuracy.

198
00:28:42,459 --> 00:28:47,005
So, with the current state of research, this is generally not very easy to do.

199
00:28:51,068 --> 00:29:03,767
Now we can call such systems displays, where the display term is a very generic term, and we can call these smell interfaces olfactory displays.

200
00:29:05,449 --> 00:29:15,545
Now they are shown to induce—if it is possible to generate such effects—then they are shown to induce strong cognitive and emotional responses from people.

201
00:29:17,487 --> 00:29:20,552
As per study, it has been found that if we can add

202
00:29:21,224 --> 00:29:33,536
this effect in our overall immersive experience along with the other senses, then that can lead to strong cognitive and emotional responses which,

203
00:29:33,536 --> 00:29:48,767
makes the experience attractive for the users, makes the experience more immersive and also there is evidence that

204
00:29:49,304 --> 00:29:54,911
pleasant odors can help reduce visually induced motion sickness or, in other words, cyber sickness.

205
00:29:55,011 --> 00:30:06,945
So, if we add a pleasant odor, then probably the effect of cyber sickness can be reduced to some extent.

206
00:30:06,965 --> 00:30:10,950
Now, let us try to have a quick look at the taste interfaces.

207
00:30:13,353 --> 00:30:16,797
Again, like smell, relatively little work

208
00:30:17,468 --> 00:30:21,053
has been done on simulating the taste electronically.

209
00:30:25,279 --> 00:30:36,574
An example system is shown here, where electrodes are placed over and under the tongue to provide stimulation that simulates the main taste types.

210
00:30:36,935 --> 00:30:39,418
So, you can understand the complexity of the setup.

211
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:45,687
You have to put electrodes over and below the tongue,

212
00:30:46,730 --> 00:30:52,317
to provide stimulation that will, in a sense, simulate the effect of taste.

213
00:30:56,021 --> 00:31:00,815
It is generally difficult to develop taste interfaces, taste displays,

214
00:31:00,815 --> 00:31:13,902
without actually causing people to eat food during the experience which of course is not a very convenient arrangement and generally quite difficult to achieve in practice.

215
00:31:17,746 --> 00:31:21,032
So that brings us to the end of this discussion.

216
00:31:22,655 --> 00:31:25,120
Let us summarize what we have learned so far.

217
00:31:27,064 --> 00:31:33,576
So in this discussion, we talked about our remaining senses other than visual and auditory senses.

218
00:31:34,238 --> 00:31:39,568
So we talked about the touch sense, proprioception, taste, and smell.

219
00:31:41,084 --> 00:31:49,533
We have seen how the human somatosensory system is related to perception of touch as well as proprioception.

220
00:31:49,953 --> 00:31:59,062
There are several receptors, out of which six are dedicated to touch, three are dedicated to proprioception and kinesthesis.

221
00:31:59,082 --> 00:32:06,670
We briefly talked about haptic explorations and how that helps us perceive the world around us in terms of

222
00:32:07,173 --> 00:32:10,998
perceiving the geometric properties of objects around us.

223
00:32:13,101 --> 00:32:25,659
And we also discussed how that support, if provided in a VR or XR setting in general, can help increase the immersion and presence.

224
00:32:25,940 --> 00:32:28,063
Similarly, we talked about taste and smell.

225
00:32:28,964 --> 00:32:36,775
We noted how little research has been done and how complex it is to basically generate the effects of smell and taste.

226
00:32:38,257 --> 00:32:41,201
through cumbersome setups.

227
00:32:42,403 --> 00:32:44,145
However, there is little work on it.

228
00:32:44,626 --> 00:32:53,538
Some research prototypes are there, but still those are not very popular or commonly used in commercially available VR systems.

229
00:32:55,941 --> 00:33:00,427
So, with that, we have completed our discussion on the human factors and XR.

230
00:33:01,428 --> 00:33:07,977
In the next lecture, we will be talking about another part of the foundations of the

231
00:33:09,205 --> 00:33:13,972
XR systems, that is, how to build interactive systems.

232
00:33:14,052 --> 00:33:16,555
So, what are the stages involved in interactive systems?

233
00:33:16,736 --> 00:33:23,686
XR as an interactive system we have already learned, and next we will be talking about interactive XR system development.

234
00:33:23,706 --> 00:33:25,108
How to develop such a system?

235
00:33:25,168 --> 00:33:26,790
What systematic process to follow?

236
00:33:28,412 --> 00:33:31,397
So, what is the theory and in practice how we can do that?

237
00:33:32,058 --> 00:33:34,521
The practice part we will cover using a case study.

238
